    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d753">

                <pb facs="0894" n="878" sameAs="frus:frus1947v08/pg878"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/12–247</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bernbaum</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                        <lb/> No. 1861</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Managua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-12-02">December 2,
                        1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to this Embassy’s telegrams
                    nos. 374 and 376 of November 27 and 29, respectively,<anchor n="84"
                        xml:id="d753-f1"/> I have the honor to submit the following preliminary
                    observations on the political situation following the departure for Mexico on
                    November 29 of the deposed president, Dr. Leonardo Argüello, after a record
                    asylum of six months and two days in the Mexican Embassy.</p>
                <p>In line with the information previously made available to this office by the
                    Mexican Embassy, Dr. Argüello’s departure had been authorized on November 27 by
                    the Constituent Assembly on terms indicating <pb facs="0895" n="879"
                        xml:id="pg879"/> that the Mexican Government had substantially yielded to
                    General Somoza’s demand for guarantees that Dr. Argüello would not be allowed to
                    take any part in political activities against the <hi rendition="#italic">de
                        facto</hi> regime.<anchor n="85" xml:id="d753-f2"/> …</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Maurice</hi> M. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Bernbaum</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d753-f1" n="84" xml:id="d753-f1-content">
                <p>Neither printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d753-f2" n="85" xml:id="d753-f2-content">
                <p>The death of Leonardo Argüello on December 14, 1947, attributed to a heart
                    attack, was reported by the Ambassador in Mexico (Thurston) in airgram A–1131,
                    December 15, 1947 (817.001 Argüello, Leonardo/12–1647).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
